<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Welcome to Tectonic Plates</h1>
    <div class="flex gap-2">
      <a href="/workouts/new" class="<%= button_style %> bg-lime-500 px-3 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Start a new workout</a>
      <%# Not on an account with no sessions at all, where it led to an empty list with
          nothing in it to continue. #636. %>
      <%- if @anything %>
        <a href="/workouts/" class="<%= button_style %> bg-sky-800 px-3 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-sky-900">Continue a workout</a>
      <%- end %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-8 flex items-center justify-between">
    <%# Paging a month is the one thing this page is tapped for, and at 51x28 it was the
        smallest target on it. min-h-11 is the 44px a thumb is aimed at; px-3 buys eight
        more across, which is what an arrow and three characters of a month name need to
        clear 44 on that axis too without the two of them closing on the heading. %>
    <a href="/?month=<%= @previous.strftime('%Y-%m') %>" rel="prev"
       class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-md px-3 py-2 text-sm text-gray-500 hover:bg-gray-100">
      &larr; <%= @previous.strftime('%b') %>
    </a>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= @month.strftime('%B %Y') %></h2>
    <a href="/?month=<%= @following.strftime('%Y-%m') %>" rel="next"
       class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-md px-3 py-2 text-sm text-gray-500 hover:bg-gray-100">
      <%= @following.strftime('%b') %> &rarr;
    </a>
  </div>

  <%# A quiet month and an account with nothing in it yet are different news. The second is
      somebody who closed Start here without acting and came back to a blank grid (#636), and
      the useful thing to tell them is where the two ways in are. %>
  <%- if @tally.empty? && !@anything %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-gray-500">
      Nothing logged or planned yet.
      <a href="/start" class="font-medium text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">Start here</a>
      explains the two ways in.
    </p>
  <%- elsif @tally.empty? %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-gray-500">Nothing trained or planned this month.</p>
  <%- else %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-gray-500">
      <%# The separator is the middle dot itself rather than `&middot;`, because the join
          happens inside an expression whose result this tag escapes, and an escaped entity
          arrives on the page spelled out rather than drawn. The month arrows further up are
          left as entities: they sit in static markup, which nothing here escapes. %>
      <%= [[:performed, 'trained'], [:planned, 'planned'], [:skipped, 'missed']]
            .filter_map { |status, word| "#{@tally[status]} #{word}" if @tally[status] }.join(' · ') %>
    </p>
  <%- end %>

  <%# A real table, not two grids of divs. #339.

      It was a grid of seven day names and, separately, a grid of thirty-odd cells, with
      nothing connecting a cell to the column above it. A screen reader read "Mon Tue Wed Thu
      Fri Sat Sun" and then a flat run of dates: no information was lost -- each cell carries
      its own date and the entries inside carry sr-only text -- but there was no way to
      navigate it as a calendar. Answering "what am I doing on Thursday" meant listening to
      the whole month and counting, where a sighted reader glances at a column.

      <th scope="col"> is the whole fix. It gives a screen reader real table navigation, so a
      cell is announced with the day it sits under, and it costs nothing visually: a table
      carries the same grid with border-separate and a one-pixel spacing, which is what the
      gap-px on a grey background was doing before.

      #339 offers role="grid" with role="columnheader" as the alternative, keeping the divs.
      The table is simpler and less to get wrong -- the roles have to be applied to every
      level and a row missing one is a grid a reader cannot move around at all.

      The rounding is on the wrapper rather than the table, because border-separate does not
      clip its cells to the table's own corners. %>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-lg">
    <table class="w-full table-fixed border-separate border-spacing-px bg-gray-200 text-sm">
      <caption class="sr-only">Training calendar for <%= @month.strftime('%B %Y') %></caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <%- Tectonic::Calendar.day_columns(@week_starts_on).each do |short, full| %>
            <th scope="col" class="bg-gray-50 py-2 text-center text-xs font-semibold leading-6 text-gray-500">
              <span aria-hidden="true"><%= short %></span>
              <span class="sr-only"><%= full %></span>
            </th>
          <%- end %>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <%- @weeks.each do |week| %>
          <tr>
          <%- week.each do |cell| %>
        <%# A day outside the month is still drawn, so a week that straddles a boundary
            reads as a week; it is just dimmed, because the month is what is being read. %>
        <%# h-20 and align-top rather than min-h-20: a table cell's height is decided by its
            row, so a minimum on the cell is not what holds an empty week open. %>
        <td class="h-20 p-1 align-top <%= cell[:in_month] ? 'bg-white' : 'bg-gray-50 text-gray-500' %>">
          <div class="text-right">
            <span class="inline-flex h-6 w-6 items-center justify-center rounded-full text-xs
                         <%= 'bg-lime-500 font-semibold text-gray-900' if cell[:today] %>">
              <%= cell[:date].day %>
            </span>
          </div>
          <%# Performed, planned and skipped are the words a workout already answers
              with. Skipped is the one a list cannot show: a missed session is simply
              absent there, and seeing the hole is the point of a calendar.

              This used to draw nothing but the tint below sm, on the reasoning that
              "planned" is forty-six pixels at text-xs and a seventh of a phone leaves about
              thirty. #492 is the screenshot of what that produces: five rows of empty
              coloured boxes, which reads as a page that failed to render rather than as a
              deliberate minimum. The legend underneath is a key to a code nobody can tell
              is a code.

              The measurement was also pessimistic. A 390px phone gives a column of about
              fifty-five and roughly forty-seven inside the padding, and the text drawn here
              is the session's own label rather than the status word -- "Squat", "Bench",
              "Ride", "Deadlift" -- which is four to eight characters and the more useful
              half besides. The tint already says trained or planned or missed; what a cell
              could not say is *which session*, and that is the thing worth the pixels.

              Truncate rather than hide, so "Accessories" becomes a word ending in an
              ellipsis instead of nothing at all. A truncated word is still a word somebody
              can recognise; an empty box is not.

              sr-only keeps the full "trained, Squat" in the accessibility tree at every
              width, which the title attribute it replaces could not do -- a title has no
              way of being reached by a thumb. min-h-11 holds the link open to one. %>
          <%# A cell holding two sessions drew the same word twice with nothing between
              them, which is #143: the morning lift and the evening walk both read
              "performed". Where a session has a name, or a program day whose focus can
              stand in for one, the chip shows that instead -- the word is what happened
              to it, the label is which one it is, and the label is the half that tells
              two apart. The word stays in the accessibility tree either way, because
              "morning lift" alone does not say whether it was trained. %>
          <%- Tectonic::Calendar.entries(cell[:workouts]).each do |entry| %>
            <a href="/workouts/<%= entry[:id] %>"
               class="mt-1 flex min-h-11 items-center justify-center rounded px-1 text-xs
                      font-medium sm:min-h-6 <%= entry[:style] %>">
              <span class="sr-only"><%= [entry[:word], entry[:label]].compact.join(', ') %></span>
              <span class="truncate" aria-hidden="true"><%= entry[:label] || entry[:word] %></span>
            </a>
          <%- end %>
        </td>
          <%- end %>
          </tr>
        <%- end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <%# On a phone this is now the key to the grid rather than a footnote to it, so each
      swatch is the chip: the same tint, the same rounded rectangle, at a size that can be
      told apart from the two beside it. The saturated dots it replaces were a colour that
      appeared nowhere in the calendar they were meant to explain. %>
  <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4 text-xs text-gray-500">
    <span class="flex items-center gap-1.5">
      <span class="inline-block h-4 w-6 rounded bg-lime-100 ring-1 ring-inset ring-lime-800/25"></span>trained
    </span>
    <span class="flex items-center gap-1.5">
      <span class="inline-block h-4 w-6 rounded bg-sky-100 ring-1 ring-inset ring-sky-800/25"></span>planned
    </span>
    <span class="flex items-center gap-1.5">
      <span class="inline-block h-4 w-6 rounded bg-rose-100 ring-1 ring-inset ring-rose-800/25"></span>missed
    </span>
  </div>
</div>
